{
  "image_name": "1ccf2da6cac617547fc03c0228a464ef.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how chromatin remodelers control nucleosome organization and, in turn, gene function. It starts from the idea that many different nucleosome arrangements are possible along DNA around a factor-binding site, with some configurations more common than others. Different remodelers then bias this landscape toward distinct preferred organizations: one arrangement blocks the site and is associated with gene repression, another exposes the site and supports gene activation, and a mutant remodeler appears to create an intermediate, only partly accessible state linked to disease.\n\nAs a whole, the figure emphasizes that chromatin remodeling is a dynamic, directional process that shapes nucleosome spacing, positioning, disassembly, and histone exchange. At promoters, remodelers can place the +1 nucleosome relative to a barrier and remove nucleosomes; across gene bodies, they help generate regular spacing and can exchange standard H2A–H2B dimers for the variant H2A.Z–H2B. These organization changes feed directly into transcriptional outcomes, shown as a feedback-like relationship between nucleosome architecture and gene expression.\n\nThe lower part broadens this concept to other DNA-based processes, indicating remodeler roles in loop extrusion with CTCF/cohesin, replication origin organization, and DNA double-strand break repair, while also depicting remodeling as ATP-driven movement between alternative nucleosome states.",
